(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xix-e"] = {"qs": [["Types of Investments", "In an interest rate swap, a fund that pays fixed and receives floating will benefit when:", ["Floating interest rates fall sharply after the swap starts", "The fixed rate is reset to a higher level by the counterparty", "Bond yields remain exactly unchanged for the full tenor", "Floating interest rates rise above the fixed rate"], 3, "Paying fixed and receiving floating gains when floating payments received exceed the fixed payments made. If floating rates fall, the fund receives less while still paying the same fixed rate."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Which body grants registration to an AIF in India?", ["Association of Mutual Funds in India (AMFI)", "Ministry of Corporate Affairs", "Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI)", "Reserve Bank of India"], 2, "AIFs are registered and regulated by SEBI under the AIF Regulations, 2012. AMFI is the mutual fund industry body, and RBI regulates banks and NBFCs."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "Which pair correctly matches an AIF strategy with its usual category?", ["Venture capital fund — Category III", "Private debt fund — Category I", "Infrastructure fund — Category III", "Long-short equity fund — Category III"], 3, "Long-short equity is a classic hedge-fund style strategy and falls in Category III. Venture capital and infrastructure funds belong to Category I, while private debt and private equity sit in Category II."], ["Types of Investments", "A fund buys a stock at ₹800 and simultaneously buys a put option with strike ₹760 for a premium of ₹15. The maximum loss per share on the combined position is:", ["₹15", "₹55", "₹40", "₹775"], 1, "Below ₹760 the put offsets further falls, so the loss is the ₹40 drop to the strike plus the ₹15 premium = ₹55. This 'protective put' caps downside at a known cost."], ["Governance of Funds and Due Diligence", "A Category III fund's compliance officer is expected to:", ["Decide the fund's asset allocation", "Approve the custodian's own audit", "Market the fund to new investors", "Monitor adherence to SEBI regulations and report non-compliance"], 3, "The compliance officer ensures that the fund follows regulations and its own documents, and reports breaches. Investment decisions and marketing are outside this role."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "The investment manager of an AIF is primarily responsible for:", ["Making investment decisions in line with the fund documents and regulations", "Safekeeping of all securities held by the fund in its demat account and settling every trade", "Certifying the fund's accounts as the statutory auditor", "Resolving investor complaints as the regulator"], 0, "The manager runs the portfolio and must follow the private placement memorandum and SEBI rules. Custody, statutory audit and regulation are separate roles."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "Two funds have the same Sharpe ratio. Fund X has a lower beta than Fund Y. For an investor holding a well-diversified main portfolio, Fund X will have a:", ["Lower Treynor ratio, regardless of excess return", "Higher standard deviation than Fund Y", "Higher Treynor ratio, if both have the same excess return", "Lower information ratio by definition"], 2, "Treynor = excess return / beta. With the same excess return, the fund with lower beta earns more per unit of market risk. Sharpe uses total risk, so the two ratios can rank funds differently."], ["Valuation", "A fund holds an OTC interest rate swap with a bank. To value it independently, the administrator would most likely use:", ["The swap's original notional amount", "The bank's own quote without any check", "The fund manager's target value", "A model based on current market yield curves"], 3, "OTC swaps have no exchange price, so they are valued by discounting expected cash flows using current market curves. Relying only on the counterparty's or manager's numbers creates conflicts."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "A Category III fund appoints an auditor who is a close relative of the fund manager. The main concern is that:", ["The fund's returns will automatically fall", "The auditor's independence may be compromised", "SEBI will cancel the fund's registration immediately", "The custodian will be unable to hold the fund's securities"], 1, "Auditors must be independent so they can challenge the manager's accounts and valuations. A close relationship creates a conflict that weakens the assurance investors receive."], ["Investment Strategies", "A volatility-selling strategy that writes options regularly tends to show:", ["Occasional large gains with frequent small losses", "No losses at all over time, because the option premiums received are always earned by the seller", "Returns that rise sharply in market crashes", "Steady small gains with occasional large losses in market shocks"], 3, "Option sellers collect premium most of the time but face large pay-outs when markets move sharply. This creates negatively skewed returns, often described as 'picking up coins in front of a steamroller'."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Leverage employed by a Category III AIF is subject to:", ["No limit at all since investors are sophisticated", "Limits set by SEBI and disclosure to investors", "Approval by the stock exchange for each trade", "A ban, since Category III cannot borrow or use derivatives"], 1, "Category III AIFs may use leverage, but SEBI caps gross exposure relative to NAV and requires disclosures of leverage to investors and the regulator."], ["Types of Investments", "A convertible bond is attractive to some hedge strategies because it:", ["Combines a fixed income claim with an embedded option to convert into equity", "Carries no credit risk at all since it is always backed by a full government guarantee", "Must be converted into shares on the date of issue", "Is a pure equity instrument with voting rights from day one"], 0, "A convertible pays interest like a bond but lets the holder convert into shares, so its value has a bond floor plus an equity option. Convertible arbitrage strategies try to exploit mispricing of that embedded option."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "A large investor negotiates a lower management fee through a side letter. A most-favoured-nation (MFN) clause would allow other investors to:", ["Cancel their commitments without notice", "Take over management of the fund", "Receive the large investor's share of profits", "Elect to receive similar terms granted to comparable investors"], 3, "An MFN clause lets eligible investors see and opt into better terms given to others of similar size or type. It addresses fairness concerns created by side letters."], ["Investment Strategies", "Company A announces it will acquire Company B at ₹500 per share in cash; B trades at ₹470. A merger arbitrage fund buys B. Its main risk is that:", ["The broad equity market rises 5% next week", "B's price moves up to exactly ₹500 on completion", "Interest rates on government bonds fall slightly", "The deal fails and B's price falls back sharply"], 3, "Merger arbitrage earns the spread if the deal completes. The key risk is deal failure from regulatory, financing or shareholder issues, which can send the target's price down well below the current price."], ["Investments Landscape", "Compared with long-only listed equity funds, alternative investment strategies typically show:", ["Daily liquidity, lower fees and fully standardised valuation", "Guaranteed returns with complete transparency of every position", "Lower minimum ticket sizes aimed at small retail savers", "Lower liquidity, higher fees and more complex valuation"], 3, "Alternatives usually carry lock-ins or redemption limits, management plus performance fees and positions that are harder to value. They are meant for sophisticated investors, not small savers, and returns are never guaranteed."], ["AIF Structuring", "The most common legal form used for AIFs in India is a:", ["Public limited company listed on an exchange", "Cooperative society registered with a state", "Sole proprietorship of the fund manager", "Trust registered under the Indian Trusts Act"], 3, "Most Indian AIFs are set up as trusts because of flexibility and familiarity with pass-through and trustee-based governance. LLPs and companies are permitted but less common."], ["Types of Investments", "A Category III fund wants to profit from a fall in the price of a stock it does not own. The standard way to do this in Indian markets is to:", ["Sell the stock's futures contract", "Buy the stock in the cash market", "Buy a call option on the stock", "Subscribe to the stock's rights issue"], 0, "Selling futures creates a short exposure that gains when the price falls. Buying the stock or a call option are bullish positions, and a rights issue adds to the holding."], ["AIF Structuring", "Why might a manager choose a close-ended rather than open-ended structure for a Category III fund holding less liquid listed small-caps?", ["To charge a higher management fee that open-ended Category III funds are not allowed to charge", "To match fund liquidity with the time needed to exit the holdings", "To avoid having any NAV computed during the tenure", "To be able to accept retail investors with small tickets"], 1, "Illiquid holdings can be hard to sell quickly to meet redemptions. A close-ended structure avoids forced selling and liquidity mismatch, while NAV reporting and investor minimums still apply."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A fund's one-day VaR is ₹2 crore. Using the square-root-of-time rule, its approximate 25-day VaR is:", ["₹10 crore", "₹50 crore", "₹25 crore", "₹4 crore"], 0, "Under the square-root-of-time rule, VaR scales with the square root of the horizon: ₹2 crore × √25 = ₹2 crore × 5 = ₹10 crore. This assumes independent, identically distributed returns."], ["Regulatory Framework", "A Category III fund wants to invest a large share of its investable funds in a single listed company. It should first check:", ["Whether the investee company has more than 1,000 employees and a long operating history", "Whether the company pays dividends every month", "The custodian's personal opinion on the stock", "SEBI's concentration limit on investment in one investee company"], 3, "SEBI imposes a concentration cap per investee company for Category III funds, expressed relative to NAV or investable funds. The fund must comply regardless of the manager's conviction."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "A fund returned 11% against a benchmark return of 9%, with tracking error of 5%. The information ratio is:", ["2.00", "0.40", "0.22", "1.80"], 1, "Information ratio = active return / tracking error = (11 − 9) / 5 = 0.40. It measures how consistently the manager adds value relative to the benchmark."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "In a Category III AIF set up as a trust, the sponsor is best described as the entity that:", ["Holds the fund's securities in safe custody on behalf of investors", "Provides audit opinions on the fund's annual financial statements", "Sets up the AIF and contributes the required continuing interest", "Acts as the exchange where the fund's units are traded"], 2, "The sponsor establishes the fund and must keep a continuing interest, aligning it with investors. Custody, audit and trading venues are provided by separate parties."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "A client with a ₹3 crore portfolio wants to put ₹2.5 crore into a single high-volatility Category III fund. The most appropriate response is to:", ["Accept the investment as proposed, since the client comfortably meets the minimum ticket size for AIFs", "Advise borrowing more money to increase the allocation further", "Recommend the fund because past returns have been strong", "Explain concentration risk and suggest a smaller allocation suited to the overall plan"], 3, "Meeting the minimum investment does not make a large concentrated allocation suitable. Putting over 80% of wealth in one volatile fund creates excessive risk, so the investor should be guided to a proportionate allocation."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A close-ended Category III fund reaches the end of its tenure with some unlisted holdings unsold. One possible exit route permitted under SEBI's framework is:", ["Selling the assets to the sponsor at any price it chooses", "In-specie distribution of the unsold assets to investors, with their consent", "Extending the fund's tenure indefinitely without seeking any approval from the investors", "Writing the assets off and charging investors a penalty"], 1, "SEBI's framework for unliquidated investments allows options such as in-specie distribution or transfer to a liquidation scheme, subject to investor consent. Unilateral extension or related-party sales at arbitrary prices are not acceptable."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "An equal-weighted index, compared with a market-cap weighted one, gives:", ["More influence to smaller companies in the index", "Almost all influence to the largest companies", "Influence based on each stock's share price", "No role to index rebalancing"], 0, "Equal weighting assigns the same weight to every constituent, so smaller companies matter more than under cap weighting. It also requires regular rebalancing to restore equal weights."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "A fund returned 14% with a downside deviation of 8%. The risk-free rate is 6%. Its Sortino ratio is:", ["1.75", "1.00", "0.75", "2.33"], 1, "Sortino = (return − risk-free) / downside deviation = (14 − 6) / 8 = 1.00. It penalises only harmful volatility, unlike the Sharpe ratio, which uses total standard deviation."], ["Investment Strategies", "A statistical arbitrage strategy typically relies on:", ["Long-term ownership of a single unlisted company", "Physical storage of agricultural commodities", "Quantitative models exploiting short-term price relationships across many securities", "Lending money to infrastructure projects for ten years or more and holding the loans to maturity"], 2, "Stat-arb uses models to identify small, temporary mispricings, often betting that prices will revert to historical relationships. Positions are many, short-dated and usually close to market-neutral."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "A 'hurdle rate' in a performance fee arrangement means:", ["The manager's management fee is waived in a falling market", "The manager earns a performance fee only if returns exceed a set minimum", "The investor must pay a fixed fee every year regardless of how the fund has actually performed", "Redemptions are allowed only above a certain NAV level"], 1, "A hurdle sets a minimum return investors must receive before carry applies. It protects investors from paying performance fees on modest returns they could earn elsewhere."], ["Investments Landscape", "A portfolio earned a nominal return of 12% in a year when inflation was 5%. Its real return, computed exactly, is closest to:", ["7.00%", "17.60%", "6.67%", "5.71%"], 2, "Real return = (1 + nominal) / (1 + inflation) − 1 = 1.12 / 1.05 − 1 = 6.67%. Simply subtracting 5 from 12 gives 7%, which is only an approximation that overstates the real return."], ["AIF Structuring", "An open-ended Category III fund receives large subscriptions immediately after a sharp NAV rise. Fair treatment of existing investors in performance-fee calculation is mainly addressed through:", ["Converting the fund to a close-ended structure", "Suspending NAV calculation for a quarter", "Paying new investors a bonus in units", "Equalisation or series accounting"], 3, "Equalisation or series accounting ensures each investor pays performance fees only on gains they actually earned. Without it, incoming investors may pay fees on gains they did not enjoy, or escape fees on later gains."], ["Valuation", "A fund changes its valuation method for unlisted bonds in mid-year, which raises NAV by 3%. Investors should expect:", ["No disclosure at all, since a fund's valuation methods are confidential business information", "Disclosure of the change, its reasons and its impact on NAV", "An automatic waiver of performance fees for ten years", "Immediate de-registration of the fund"], 1, "Valuation methods should be applied consistently, and any change must be justified and disclosed to investors. Unexplained changes that lift NAV may hide performance problems."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "A fund's documents permit the manager to 'gate' redemptions at 15% of NAV per quarter. During a crisis, investors ask for 40% of NAV. The likely outcome is:", ["All redemptions are paid in full immediately", "The fund is wound up automatically", "Redemptions are scaled back proportionately, with the rest carried forward", "Only the earliest requests received are paid in full and all the later requests are cancelled"], 2, "A gate limits total redemptions in a period to protect remaining investors from fire sales. Requests above the gate are usually met pro rata and carried forward to later periods."], ["Governance of Funds and Due Diligence", "A fund manager's employees trade personally in stocks the fund also trades. The best governance control is:", ["Allowing staff to trade freely if they disclose annually", "Banning only the fund manager from personal trading", "Requiring staff to trade only after the fund's NAV rises", "Pre-clearance of personal trades and restricted lists"], 3, "Pre-clearance and restricted lists prevent front-running and misuse of fund information. Annual disclosure alone comes too late to stop conflicts arising."], ["Types of Investments", "An option's 'time value' is best defined as:", ["The full premium paid for an at-the-money option", "The part of the premium above the option's intrinsic value", "The difference between strike price and futures price", "The interest earned on the margin money deposited with the broker until expiry"], 1, "Option premium = intrinsic value + time value. Time value reflects the chance that the option becomes more valuable before expiry, and it decays to zero by the expiry date."], ["Governance of Funds and Due Diligence", "Which of the following is a red flag in due diligence of a Category III fund?", ["The fund uses an established custodian and independent administrator", "The fund has a written personal trading policy for staff", "The manager uses a small, unknown auditor and refuses to share audited accounts", "The fund's risk management team reports to someone other than the portfolio manager who takes positions"], 2, "An obscure auditor and refusal to share audited statements prevent verification of assets and returns. The other choices are signs of good governance and independence."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "Investors receive a report showing that a fund's leverage rose from 1.2x to 1.9x NAV in one quarter. A sensible investor action is to:", ["Ignore it because leverage always improves returns", "Redeem immediately without asking any questions, since higher leverage always means fraud", "Ask the manager why leverage rose and whether it fits the stated strategy", "Ask the custodian to reduce leverage directly"], 2, "A sharp rise in leverage may signal style drift or increased risk. Investors should seek explanation and check consistency with the PPM before deciding on action."], ["Taxation", "Securities Transaction Tax (STT) is relevant to a Category III fund because it is:", ["A tax on the fund manager's personal salary", "Levied on purchases and sales of listed securities and derivatives on exchanges", "Payable only on unlisted debentures", "A tax collected on investors' redemptions from bank fixed deposits and savings accounts"], 1, "STT is charged on exchange-traded transactions in equity shares, equity-oriented units and derivatives. It adds to trading costs, which matters for high-turnover strategies."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "Index 'reconstitution' refers to:", ["Recalculating the index only for dividends", "Converting a price return index into a currency-adjusted index for overseas investors", "Suspending the index during market holidays", "Changing the list of constituents according to the index rules"], 3, "Reconstitution periodically adds and removes constituents based on criteria such as size and liquidity. Rebalancing adjusts weights of existing constituents. Both affect funds tracking the index."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A fund's one-day 99% VaR is ₹5 crore. This means:", ["On 1 day in 100, losses are expected to exceed ₹5 crore", "The fund can never lose more than ₹5 crore in a day", "The fund will lose ₹5 crore on 99 days out of 100", "The fund's average daily loss is ₹5 crore"], 0, "VaR gives a loss threshold at a confidence level. At 99%, losses should exceed ₹5 crore on about 1% of days. VaR does not show how large those tail losses could be."], ["Investments Landscape", "An investor needs a large part of her portfolio to pay for her daughter's overseas education in 18 months. Which constraint should dominate any decision to invest in a Category III AIF?", ["Her preference for funds with famous managers", "The fund's past three-year return compared with peers", "Liquidity needs and the short time horizon", "Whether the fund invests mainly in large-cap stocks"], 2, "Money needed within 18 months should not be locked in vehicles with lock-ins, gates or volatile returns. Liquidity and horizon are binding constraints; past returns or manager reputation cannot override them."], ["Valuation", "The 'fair value hierarchy' gives greatest reliability to valuations based on:", ["Management's internal estimates with no observable inputs", "Prices of different assets in inactive markets", "Quoted prices in active markets for identical assets", "The original cost of the investment"], 2, "Level 1 inputs — quoted prices for identical assets in active markets — are the most reliable. Level 3 inputs rely on unobservable assumptions and need greater scrutiny."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "A good benchmark for a Category III fund should be:", ["Chosen after the year-end, based on whichever index the fund managed to beat during that year", "The same broad equity index for every type of AIF strategy", "Changed every quarter to show the fund in the best light", "Specified in advance, measurable and appropriate to the fund's strategy"], 3, "A valid benchmark is unambiguous, investable, measurable and set before performance is evaluated. Choosing or changing it after the fact misleads investors about the manager's skill."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "The private placement memorandum of a Category III fund is primarily meant to:", ["Guarantee that the fund will meet its target return", "Disclose the strategy, risks, fees, conflicts and terms to prospective investors", "Replace the need for any trust deed, contribution agreement or investment management agreement", "Serve as an advertisement to the general public"], 1, "The PPM is the main disclosure document for an AIF. It must be balanced and comprehensive, cannot promise returns, and AIFs cannot be marketed to the general public."], ["Governance of Funds and Due Diligence", "Why is cyber-security and data protection now part of operational due diligence?", ["Because SEBI requires all AIFs to trade only online", "Breaches can lead to theft of assets, data or trading strategies", "Because cyber-security improves investment returns directly", "Because it replaces the need for a custodian"], 1, "Funds hold sensitive investor data and proprietary models, and payment fraud is a real risk. Weak cyber controls can cause losses that have nothing to do with market risk."], ["Investments Landscape", "A Category III fund states that its objective is to deliver positive returns in both rising and falling markets. This is best described as:", ["An absolute return mandate", "A relative return mandate measured only against the Nifty 50", "A passive index-replication mandate with low tracking error", "A capital protection mandate backed by a bank guarantee"], 0, "An absolute return mandate targets positive returns regardless of market direction, often using shorting and hedging. A relative return mandate only seeks to beat a benchmark, even if the benchmark itself falls."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "A prime broker typically provides a Category III fund with:", ["Statutory audit and tax filing for the fund's investors", "Registration of the fund with SEBI and filing of all regulatory reports on the manager's behalf", "Financing, securities lending and consolidated clearing services", "Independent valuation of unlisted securities for NAV purposes"], 2, "Prime brokers bundle services such as margin financing, stock borrowing for short sales, clearing and reporting. Audit, registration and independent valuation are performed by other parties."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "Investor rights to remove the manager 'without cause' usually require:", ["A decision by the fund's custodian alone", "Approval by a specified super-majority of investors", "Consent of the stock exchange", "No approval, since any single investor can do it"], 1, "Removal without cause is a serious step, so documents typically require a high percentage of investor commitments to agree. This balances investor protection with stability of management."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "Side letters with preferential terms for some investors raise the concern that:", ["Other investors may be disadvantaged without knowing the terms", "The fund's NAV cannot be computed", "The PPM becomes legally void", "The fund can no longer use leverage"], 0, "Preferential liquidity or information rights may harm other investors, for example by letting some exit earlier. SEBI expects disclosure of such arrangements and fair treatment of all investors."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "Before signing a contribution agreement, an investor should carefully check the 'indemnification' clause because it determines:", ["The fund's annual target return", "The list of stocks the fund will buy", "When the fund bears costs of claims against the manager", "The custodian's fee structure"], 2, "Indemnification can require the fund, and therefore investors, to pay legal costs and losses arising from claims against the manager. Investors negotiate carve-outs for fraud, gross negligence or wilful misconduct."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "Survivorship bias in a hedge-fund database index tends to:", ["Overstate the average returns of the hedge-fund universe", "Understate returns because only failed funds are included", "Have no effect because all funds report every month", "Reduce the measured volatility to zero"], 0, "Funds that close, often after poor performance, drop out of databases, leaving mostly survivors. The index then looks better than the true experience of investors across all funds."], ["Taxation", "GST is generally levied on:", ["Management and performance fees charged by the AIF manager", "The investor's capital contribution to the fund", "The sale proceeds of listed shares", "Dividends received by the fund"], 0, "Fund management is a service, so GST applies to fees charged by the manager. Capital contributions, share sale proceeds and dividends are not taxable supplies for GST."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "When should money-weighted return (IRR) rather than time-weighted return be used to judge performance?", ["When the manager controls the timing of investor cash flows, as in drawdown funds", "When investors themselves decide when to subscribe and redeem, as in a typical open-ended fund", "When comparing two funds with exactly identical cash flows each day", "When the fund has no cash flows during the whole year"], 0, "IRR reflects the timing and size of cash flows. In close-ended drawdown funds, the manager decides when to call and return money, so IRR is appropriate. TWR suits open-ended funds where investors control flows."], ["Valuation", "A fund holds a large block of a small-cap stock equal to ten days' average trading volume. A conservative valuation practice might be to:", ["Value it at a premium to the market price because the fund's stake in the company is large", "Exclude it from NAV until it is sold", "Use the highest price of the past year", "Consider a liquidity adjustment if the block cannot be sold at the quoted price"], 3, "The quoted price may not be achievable for a large position. Valuation policies may permit a liquidity discount, applied consistently and disclosed, to reflect realistic exit value."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "A fund's NAV peaked at ₹150, fell to ₹120, then recovered to ₹135. A high-water mark of ₹150 means the manager can charge a performance fee:", ["On the ₹15 recovery from ₹120 to ₹135", "On any positive return in the calendar year", "Only on gains after NAV rises above ₹150", "On the full NAV every quarter"], 2, "A high-water mark ensures performance fees are paid only on new highs, so investors do not pay twice for recovering earlier losses. The ₹120 to ₹135 recovery attracts no performance fee."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "In a free-float market capitalisation weighted index, a company with large promoter holding will have a weight that is:", ["Higher than its weight by total market capitalisation", "Equal for all companies in the index", "Based only on its share price", "Lower than its weight by total market capitalisation"], 3, "Free-float weighting counts only shares available for trading, excluding promoter and locked-in holdings. Companies with high promoter stakes therefore get a smaller weight than total market cap suggests."], ["Types of Investments", "Treasury bills are often held by Category III funds alongside derivative positions mainly because they:", ["Are near-cash, low-risk instruments useful as margin and liquidity buffers", "Pay the highest coupon among all fixed income instruments issued in India today", "Are equity-linked and increase the fund's market exposure", "Cannot be pledged and therefore protect the fund from margin calls"], 0, "T-bills are short-term sovereign instruments with minimal credit and interest rate risk, making them suitable collateral and cash management tools. They are issued at a discount and carry no coupon at all."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "A distributor of a Category III AIF learns that the fund pays higher trail commission than another suitable fund. The distributor should:", ["Recommend the higher-paying fund without telling the client", "Rebate part of the commission secretly to win the client", "Recommend based on client suitability and disclose the commission received", "Avoid discussing fees altogether because AIF investors are sophisticated and can find out for themselves"], 2, "Distributors must put suitability first and disclose the commission they earn so the investor can judge any conflict. Hidden commissions or secret rebates breach conduct norms."], ["Taxation", "For a Category III AIF set up as a determinate trust, where shares of beneficiaries are known, the tax is generally:", ["Waived entirely because trusts are charitable", "Paid by the fund's custodian from its own funds, since the custodian holds the trust's assets", "Assessed on the trustee as representative assessee in respect of each beneficiary's share", "Postponed until the fund is wound up"], 2, "For determinate trusts, the trustee is taxed as representative assessee in the same way and to the same extent as the beneficiaries would be. Indeterminate trusts can face tax at the maximum marginal rate."], ["Modern Portfolio Theory and Capital Market Theory", "Under CAPM, which risk is rewarded with a higher expected return?", ["Company-specific risk that can be diversified away", "Total risk measured by standard deviation alone", "Systematic risk measured by beta", "Liquidity risk of the investor's own bank account"], 2, "CAPM assumes investors hold diversified portfolios, so only non-diversifiable market risk earns a premium. Unsystematic risk can be eliminated by diversification and so earns no extra return."], ["Investments Landscape", "Which of the following is a real asset rather than a financial asset?", ["A listed corporate bond of a logistics company", "Units of a liquid mutual fund scheme", "A fixed deposit receipt issued by a scheduled bank", "A warehouse leased out to a logistics company"], 3, "A real asset is a physical or tangible asset that produces value directly, such as property, land or machinery. Bonds, mutual fund units and deposits are financial claims on someone else's assets or cash flows."], ["Modern Portfolio Theory and Capital Market Theory", "The Security Market Line shows a stock's required return of 12%, but analysts expect 15%. According to CAPM, the stock is:", ["Overvalued, with a negative alpha of 3%", "Correctly priced because it lies on the CML", "Risk-free because its return exceeds the requirement", "Undervalued, with a positive alpha of 3%"], 3, "When expected return exceeds the CAPM-required return, the stock plots above the SML and appears underpriced. The 3% excess is its alpha. That does not make the stock risk-free."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "An investor wants exposure to a strategy that profits mainly from mispricing between cash and futures markets. The most relevant AIF category is:", ["Category III, which covers trading and arbitrage strategies", "Category I, which covers venture capital and SME funds", "Category II, which covers private equity and private debt", "A Social Impact Fund under Category I"], 0, "Cash-futures arbitrage is a trading strategy in listed markets, which falls within Category III. Categories I and II mainly invest in unlisted companies with long holding periods."], ["Types of Investments", "A fund sells (writes) a call option on a stock it does not own. The theoretical maximum loss on this position is:", ["Limited to the premium received at the time of writing", "Limited to the strike price of the option", "Zero, because the option seller always keeps the premium", "Unlimited, because the stock price can keep rising"], 3, "A naked call writer must deliver the stock at the strike price however high the market price goes, so losses have no upper limit. The premium received is the writer's maximum gain, not the maximum loss."], ["Valuation", "Why does a fund's valuation committee often include members outside the investment team?", ["To reduce the conflict arising because fees depend on NAV", "To make NAV calculation faster each day", "To avoid sharing valuations with auditors", "Because portfolio managers are not allowed to see prices"], 0, "Managers earn management and performance fees based on NAV, so they have an incentive to value high. Independent members help ensure valuations are objective and defensible."], ["Taxation", "A key tax difference between Category III AIFs and Category I/II AIFs is that:", ["Category III funds are fully exempt from income tax", "Category III income is always taxed only in investors' hands", "Category III funds do not have statutory pass-through status", "Category I and II funds pay tax at the maximum marginal rate"], 2, "The Income-tax Act grants pass-through to Category I and II AIFs for non-business income. Category III funds lack this, so tax is generally paid at fund level, often through trustee-level taxation."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "Liquidity monitoring of an open-ended Category III fund mainly compares:", ["The fund's returns against the Nifty 50", "How quickly holdings can be sold against likely redemption demands", "The manager's salary against the fee income", "The number of investors against the custodian's capacity"], 1, "Funds must be able to meet redemptions without fire sales. Liquidity analysis maps the time needed to liquidate positions against expected and stressed outflows."], ["AIF Structuring", "In a master-feeder structure, the feeder fund:", ["Pools money from investors and invests it into the master fund", "Makes all trades directly while the master only holds cash", "Acts as the custodian for the master fund's assets", "Lends money to the master fund at a fixed rate"], 0, "Feeders collect capital, often from different investor groups, and channel it into one master fund that runs the portfolio. This allows one strategy to serve several investor types efficiently."], ["Regulatory Framework", "An AIF scheme (other than an angel fund) cannot have more than how many investors?", ["1,000", "200", "50", "10,000"], 0, "SEBI caps each AIF scheme at 1,000 investors, keeping AIFs as private pools. Angel funds have a lower limit of 200 investors."], ["Modern Portfolio Theory and Capital Market Theory", "Arbitrage Pricing Theory and multi-factor models extend CAPM mainly by:", ["Allowing several systematic risk factors to explain expected returns", "Assuming that the market portfolio is the one and only source of priced risk for all assets", "Removing the need for any risk-free rate in pricing", "Proving that diversification increases unsystematic risk"], 0, "Multi-factor models use factors such as size, value, momentum or macro variables, each with its own sensitivity. CAPM uses only one factor, the market portfolio."], ["AIF Structuring", "A parallel fund structure is generally used when:", ["The manager wants to avoid all investor reporting", "Different investor groups need separate vehicles that invest side by side", "The fund wants to invest only in a single stock", "Investors want to redeem their units every day without giving any notice to the manager"], 1, "Parallel funds invest alongside the main fund in proportion, often to accommodate tax, regulatory or currency needs of particular investors. They do not reduce reporting obligations."], ["Valuation", "SEBI expects an AIF's valuation of investments to be carried out by:", ["Any investor who holds the most units", "The distributor who sold the fund", "The fund's tax consultant only", "An independent valuer as per the valuation policy in the fund documents"], 3, "SEBI requires valuations by independent valuers following the methodology disclosed in the placement memorandum. Distributors and investors have conflicts and lack the mandate."], ["Governance of Funds and Due Diligence", "Why do investors prefer a fund's risk management function to be independent of portfolio managers?", ["So that portfolio managers can override the risk limits whenever markets become volatile", "So that the fund can avoid regulatory reporting", "So that risk limits can be enforced without pressure from those taking the risk", "So that the risk team can pick the stocks"], 2, "An independent risk function can challenge positions and escalate breaches. If it reports to the portfolio manager, limits may be ignored when they are most needed."], ["Governance of Funds and Due Diligence", "A manager runs two funds with different fee rates and allocates the best IPO allotments to the higher-fee fund. This is a breach of:", ["The custodian's safekeeping duties", "Fair trade allocation among clients", "The auditor's independence requirement", "The fund's leverage limit"], 1, "Managers must allocate trades fairly according to a pre-defined policy. Favouring the fund that pays more fees harms the other fund's investors and is a conflict of interest."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "A long-short equity fund that is typically 40% net long could reasonably construct a blended benchmark of:", ["40% equity index TRI and 60% money-market index", "100% equity index TRI with double leverage", "100% gold price index", "60% equity index and 40% real estate index"], 0, "A blended benchmark matching the fund's typical net exposure gives a fairer test of stock-selection skill. A full equity benchmark would penalise or flatter the fund depending on market direction."], ["Regulatory Framework", "SEBI requires certain key investment team members of an AIF manager to obtain which certification?", ["The relevant NISM certification for AIF managers", "A certificate from the Bar Council of India", "An insurance agent licence from IRDAI", "A chartered accountancy membership only"], 0, "SEBI has made an NISM certification on AIF management mandatory for key investment team members of AIF managers, so they understand the regulations, strategies and risks. Other professional licences do not satisfy this requirement."], ["Taxation", "A non-resident investor in a Category III AIF should check a tax treaty mainly because it may:", ["Allow the investor to skip KYC requirements", "Affect how the investor's income from India is taxed or credited at home", "Guarantee that the fund will never make a loss on the investor's capital in any year", "Let the investor avoid SEBI's minimum investment"], 1, "Double taxation avoidance agreements allocate taxing rights and provide relief for tax paid in India. They do not affect KYC, minimum investment or investment risk."], ["Valuation", "A listed stock held by the fund is suspended from trading for two months. The most appropriate valuation approach is to:", ["Keep using the last traded price indefinitely without any review until trading in the stock resumes", "Value it at zero immediately in every case", "Value it at the fund's purchase price", "Fair value it using a documented method, such as recent fundamentals or comparables"], 3, "A suspended stock's last price may be stale. The valuation policy should require fair valuation using reasonable inputs, with documentation and oversight, rather than mechanical rules."], ["Governance of Funds and Due Diligence", "Operational due diligence on a Category III fund focuses mainly on:", ["Controls, service providers, valuation and cash-movement processes", "The fund's expected return for the next year", "The manager's views on the economy", "Which stocks the manager plans to buy next"], 0, "Operational due diligence checks whether the fund's infrastructure can protect assets and produce reliable numbers. Many hedge fund failures stem from operational weaknesses rather than poor investment ideas."], ["Types of Investments", "A fund buys 1 lot of index futures (lot value ₹15 lakh) by paying an initial margin of ₹2 lakh. If the index rises 4%, the gain as a percentage of margin is closest to:", ["4%", "8%", "30%", "15%"], 2, "Gain = 4% × ₹15 lakh = ₹60,000. As a share of the ₹2 lakh margin, that is 30%. This shows how futures provide leveraged exposure: a small margin controls a much larger notional value."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "A key suitability risk of recommending a leveraged Category III fund to a client is that:", ["Leverage always guarantees higher returns than the benchmark", "Leverage removes the need for any risk management because borrowed money always boosts the final return", "Losses can be magnified beyond what the client expects from the market move", "Leveraged funds cannot ever fall below their initial NAV"], 2, "Leverage magnifies both gains and losses. A client who does not understand this can face drawdowns larger than the underlying market fall, so suitability assessment must cover loss-bearing capacity."], ["Valuation", "If a Category III fund overstates its NAV, which group is harmed directly?", ["Only the fund's custodian", "Nobody, because NAV errors cancel out", "Remaining and incoming investors, while redeeming investors are overpaid", "Only the stock exchange"], 2, "An inflated NAV pays redeeming investors more than their fair share and charges new investors too much, leaving the fund poorer. It can also lead to excess performance fees."], ["Modern Portfolio Theory and Capital Market Theory", "A fund has ₹100 crore, with 70% in a portfolio of beta 1.2 and the rest in cash (beta 0). It then sells index futures worth ₹40 crore. The fund's approximate beta becomes:", ["0.84", "1.20", "0.40", "0.44"], 3, "Equity beta exposure = 0.7 × 1.2 = 0.84. Short futures of ₹40 crore on ₹100 crore reduce beta by 0.40. Net beta = 0.84 − 0.40 = 0.44."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "A gross return of 20% is subject to a 2% management fee and then a 20% performance fee on the remaining return (no hurdle). The approximate net return is:", ["16.0%", "14.4%", "18.0%", "12.0%"], 1, "After the management fee, return = 20 − 2 = 18%. Performance fee = 20% × 18 = 3.6%. Net = 18 − 3.6 = 14.4%. This simplified sequence ignores timing and compounding of fee accruals."], ["Taxation", "Income from frequent trading in futures and options by a Category III fund is most likely to be treated as:", ["Long-term capital gains", "Business income", "Income from house property", "Exempt agricultural income"], 1, "Derivative trading carried out as an activity is generally treated as non-speculative business income. That characterisation affects the applicable rate and how losses are set off."], ["Fee Structure and Fund Performance", "A fund's NAV goes from ₹100 to ₹140 and then falls to ₹105. The maximum drawdown is:", ["35%", "5%", "25%", "40%"], 2, "Maximum drawdown is the largest peak-to-trough fall: (140 − 105) / 140 = 25%. It is measured from the peak, not the starting NAV, so it is not 35% or 5%."], ["Investment Strategies", "A global macro manager believes the RBI will cut rates more than the market expects. A consistent trade would be to:", ["Buy long-duration government bonds", "Sell long-duration government bonds", "Buy floating-rate notes only", "Short bank fixed deposits"], 0, "If rates fall more than expected, bond yields fall and long-duration bond prices rise most. Selling bonds or buying floating-rate notes would not benefit from an unexpected rate cut."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "Expected shortfall (conditional VaR) is best described as:", ["The average loss in the worst cases beyond the VaR threshold", "The smallest loss the fund can make in a year", "The loss on the single best day of the year", "The fund's expected return minus its fees"], 0, "Expected shortfall averages the losses in the tail beyond VaR, giving a better view of extreme risk. It is increasingly preferred because it captures tail severity that VaR ignores."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "Which investor is generally most suitable for a market-neutral Category III fund?", ["A retiree who needs a fixed monthly income and full safety of capital from the investment", "A family office seeking returns with low correlation to equity markets", "A first-time investor with ₹50,000 in savings", "A college student who wants daily liquidity for expenses"], 1, "Market-neutral funds aim to generate returns that are independent of market direction, which suits sophisticated investors wanting diversification. They need high minimum ticket sizes and are not suitable for investors who need capital safety or regular income."], ["AIF Structuring", "A close-ended Category III fund calls only 30% of committed capital at the first close. The remaining commitments will be drawn:", ["Automatically from the investor's bank account each month", "Only if the fund's NAV falls below the first-close NAV", "Through later drawdown notices as investment opportunities arise", "From the sponsor, since investors are required to pay only the first instalment of their commitment"], 2, "Commitment-based funds draw capital in stages via drawdown notices. Investors are legally bound to meet calls up to their commitment; the sponsor does not cover investor shortfalls."], ["Investments Landscape", "Under SEBI's accredited investor framework, accreditation certificates are issued by:", ["The AIF manager after reviewing the investor's bank statements", "The Income Tax Department through the annual tax return", "Accreditation agencies recognised for this purpose by SEBI", "Any registered stock broker with whom the investor trades"], 2, "SEBI designates accreditation agencies (subsidiaries of depositories and exchanges) to verify income or net worth and issue accreditation. A fund manager cannot self-certify an investor as accredited."], ["AIF Structuring", "Issuing different classes of units within a Category III fund is commonly used to:", ["Avoid disclosing the portfolio to any investor", "Give some investors a guaranteed share of profits", "Bypass the minimum investment requirement", "Offer different fee structures or terms to different investor groups"], 3, "Unit classes allow differences in fees, currency or lock-in while sharing a portfolio. They must be disclosed and cannot be used to guarantee returns or evade regulatory minimums."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "Why does SEBI require a custodian to be appointed for AIFs?", ["To decide which stocks the fund should buy or sell", "To keep the fund's securities separate from the manager for investor protection", "To guarantee investors against any fall in NAV", "To market the fund's units to new investors"], 1, "Independent custody reduces the risk that assets are misused, as the manager cannot directly control safekeeping. The custodian does not make investment decisions or guarantee returns."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "A Category III AIF is distinguished from Category I and II AIFs mainly because it:", ["May use leverage and complex trading strategies, including in listed securities and derivatives", "Is required to invest only in start-ups and social ventures", "Can only invest in unlisted debt of infrastructure companies and in government-approved social sector projects", "Is not allowed to raise money from domestic investors at all"], 0, "Category III covers funds that employ diverse or complex trading strategies and may employ leverage, such as hedge funds and long-short funds. Category I covers start-up, social and infrastructure-focused funds, and Category II covers private equity and private debt."], ["AIF Structuring", "A Category III AIF launches a new scheme under its existing registration. Which document must be filed with SEBI for the new scheme?", ["A red herring prospectus for listing the scheme's units on the main board of a stock exchange", "A scheme information document approved by AMFI", "A fresh certificate of incorporation from the ROC", "A placement memorandum for the scheme, filed through a merchant banker"], 3, "AIF schemes are launched through a private placement memorandum, filed with SEBI via a merchant banker. Prospectuses and scheme information documents relate to public issues and mutual funds."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "A cash-futures arbitrage Category III fund benchmarks itself against the Nifty 50 TRI. Why is this inappropriate?", ["The fund's returns resemble money-market returns, not equity market returns", "The Nifty 50 TRI does not include dividends", "Arbitrage funds are not allowed by SEBI to use or disclose any benchmark index at all", "The Nifty 50 contains only 10 stocks"], 0, "An arbitrage fund locks in the spread between cash and futures, giving low-risk, debt-like returns. Comparing it with an equity index is misleading; a liquid or arbitrage index is more suitable. The TRI does include dividends."], ["Modern Portfolio Theory and Capital Market Theory", "Many hedge-fund return series show negative skewness and fat tails. The main implication for risk measurement is that:", ["Standard deviation alone may understate the chance of large losses", "The Sharpe ratio becomes a perfect and complete measure of the fund's risk-adjusted return", "Beta to the equity market will always be exactly zero", "Returns can be assumed to follow a normal distribution"], 0, "Fat tails mean extreme outcomes happen more often than a normal distribution predicts, and negative skew means big losses are more likely than big gains. Measures relying on standard deviation can therefore mislead."], ["Regulatory Framework", "An AIF may raise money only through:", ["Public issue of units through a prospectus", "Private placement to eligible investors", "Daily retail SIPs through bank branches", "Unsolicited cold-calls to the general public"], 1, "AIFs are privately pooled vehicles and must raise money through private placement using a placement memorandum. Public advertising and retail offerings are not permitted."], ["Modern Portfolio Theory and Capital Market Theory", "The risk-free rate is 6%, the expected market return is 13% and a stock's beta is 0.8. Under CAPM, its required return is:", ["10.4%", "13.0%", "11.6%", "16.4%"], 2, "CAPM: required return = Rf + beta × (Rm − Rf) = 6% + 0.8 × 7% = 6% + 5.6% = 11.6%. A beta below 1 implies a return below the market's 13%."], ["Investment Strategies", "Managed futures or CTA-style strategies typically make money by:", ["Collecting rental income from commercial property", "Following price trends across futures markets, both long and short", "Buying start-ups and holding them for a decade", "Holding only fixed deposits until maturity"], 1, "Trend-following strategies go long rising markets and short falling ones across equity, bond, currency and commodity futures. They have historically performed well in sustained trending periods, including some crises."], ["Taxation", "Why does the characterisation of a Category III fund's equity gains as capital gains or business income matter?", ["Business income is completely exempt from tax in India", "Capital gains are taxed only if the fund is listed abroad, while business income is never taxed in India", "Capital gains on listed equity can attract concessional rates, while business income is taxed at normal rates", "There is no difference since both are taxed identically"], 2, "Capital gains on listed equity enjoy specific rates, often lower than the slab or maximum rates on business income. Funds therefore document their intention and holding pattern carefully."], ["Investment Strategies", "A fund has NAV ₹200 crore, long positions of ₹240 crore and short positions of ₹160 crore. Its gross exposure as a percentage of NAV is:", ["40%", "120%", "200%", "80%"], 2, "Gross exposure = (long + short) / NAV = (240 + 160) / 200 = 200%. Net exposure would be (240 − 160) / 200 = 40%. Gross shows total risk and leverage, net shows directional bias."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Under the AIF Regulations, the minimum investment for an investor (other than accredited investors and certain employees/directors) is:", ["₹10 lakh", "₹50 lakh", "₹25 crore", "₹1 crore"], 3, "The general minimum is ₹1 crore per investor, with a lower ₹25 lakh limit for employees or directors of the AIF or its manager. Accredited investors can be exempted. ₹50 lakh is the PMS minimum."], ["Investments Landscape", "Why do investors generally demand a 'liquidity premium' from alternative investments?", ["Because SEBI mandates a minimum return for all alternative funds", "Because liquid investments are always taxed at higher rates in India", "They accept that money may be hard to withdraw quickly at fair value", "Because illiquid assets have no price volatility at all"], 2, "When capital can be locked in or sold only at a discount, investors expect extra return as compensation. No regulation guarantees AIF returns, and illiquid assets still carry risk even if prices are observed less often."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "The role of the fund administrator in an open-ended Category III fund includes:", ["Selecting the fund's long and short positions every day", "Providing leverage to the fund through margin loans", "Approving the fund's registration as an AIF", "Maintaining fund accounts and processing subscriptions and redemptions"], 3, "Administrators handle accounting, investor records, capital activity and NAV-related work. Portfolio decisions belong to the manager, financing comes from the prime broker and registration is granted by SEBI."], ["Investment Strategies", "A long-short equity fund is 120% long and 90% short. If the whole market falls 10% and both books move with the market, the approximate impact on NAV is:", ["−10%", "−3%", "−12%", "+9%"], 1, "Net exposure = 120% − 90% = 30%. A 10% market fall costs about 30% × 10% = 3% of NAV. The long book loses 12% of NAV and the short book gains 9%, netting to −3%."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "Why are AIFs restricted to investors with large minimum commitments?", ["To limit participation to investors who can bear complex risks and illiquidity", "To ensure that the returns delivered by AIFs are always higher than those of comparable mutual funds", "Because AIFs are exempt from all SEBI regulation", "To allow AIFs to be listed on the main board of exchanges"], 0, "High minimum ticket sizes act as a filter for sophistication and capacity to absorb losses. AIFs remain SEBI-regulated, and a high ticket size does not imply superior returns."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "Investors in a Category III fund worry that the manager may materially change the fund's strategy after they invest. SEBI's framework addresses this by requiring that:", ["The manager may change the strategy freely as long as returns stay positive", "Only the custodian needs to be told about any change in investment strategy", "Strategy changes are banned for the full tenure, even with unanimous approval", "Material changes need investor consent, and dissenters get an exit option"], 3, "Changes to material terms of the placement memorandum require approval from a specified majority of investors, and those who disagree must be offered an exit. This protects investors who chose the fund for its stated strategy."], ["Investment Strategies", "A fund buys a 10-year government bond and sells a 5-year government bond expecting the yield curve to flatten. This is an example of:", ["Fixed income relative value", "Merger arbitrage", "Long-only small-cap investing", "Distressed equity investing"], 0, "Relative value trades take offsetting positions in related instruments to profit from changes in their relationship, here the spread between 10-year and 5-year yields, rather than the direction of rates."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "Which is a key limitation of Value at Risk?", ["It says nothing about the size of losses beyond the threshold", "It cannot be calculated for equity portfolios", "It always overstates risk in calm markets", "It requires the fund to hold no derivatives, short positions or leverage at the time of calculation"], 0, "VaR tells you the threshold but not how bad the tail can get. Expected shortfall (conditional VaR) and stress tests complement it by looking at losses beyond VaR."], ["Modern Portfolio Theory and Capital Market Theory", "Portfolios lying below the efficient frontier are:", ["Unattainable, because no combination of the available assets can ever produce them", "Sub-optimal, because another portfolio offers higher return for the same risk", "Optimal for investors who are highly risk-averse", "Always made up only of the risk-free asset"], 1, "The efficient frontier shows the best return for each level of risk. Anything below it is dominated. Portfolios above the frontier are the ones that cannot be achieved."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Investors in an AIF who have a grievance not resolved by the manager may approach:", ["Only the civil court located in the city where the fund's trust is registered", "SEBI's SCORES platform and, thereafter, online dispute resolution", "The Reserve Bank of India's ombudsman", "The fund's distributor as final authority"], 1, "SEBI-registered AIFs are covered by SCORES, and investors can escalate further through the online dispute resolution mechanism. RBI's ombudsman covers banking services, not AIFs."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A stress test that applies the market moves of a past crisis to today's portfolio is called:", ["Monte Carlo simulation", "Historical scenario analysis", "Back-testing of a trading signal", "Attribution analysis"], 1, "Historical scenarios replay real past events, such as a market crash, to see how the current portfolio would fare. Monte Carlo uses random simulations, and attribution explains past returns."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "Concentrating all prime brokerage with a single provider exposes a fund to:", ["Market risk from a sudden fall in the Nifty index", "Counterparty risk if that provider faces financial distress", "Inflation risk on the fund's cash holdings", "Currency risk on a purely rupee portfolio"], 1, "If the sole prime broker fails, the fund's collateral and positions may be frozen. Many funds use multiple prime brokers to diversify this counterparty exposure."], ["Taxation", "Because Category III tax rules and rates change with Union Budgets, the most reliable practice for an investor is to:", ["Assume last year's rates will apply forever", "Check the current law and the fund's tax disclosures before investing", "Rely on informal opinions shared on social media", "Ignore taxes completely, since they never make any real difference to net returns over time"], 1, "Tax can materially change post-tax returns. Investors should refer to current provisions and the PPM's tax section, and take professional advice where needed."], ["Investments Landscape", "The hedge fund structure is commonly traced back to Alfred Winslow Jones, whose fund combined:", ["Only government bonds held to maturity", "Commodity futures and physical gold storage", "Long stock positions, short sales and leverage", "Real estate rental income and infrastructure loans"], 2, "Jones bought stocks he liked, shorted stocks he disliked to reduce market exposure, and used leverage to magnify stock-picking returns. This 'hedged' long-short idea gave hedge funds their name."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "A 'key person' clause in an AIF's documents typically gives investors the right to:", ["Suspend new investments or exit if named key managers leave the fund", "Appoint their own fund manager every year", "Receive a fixed dividend every quarter", "Avoid paying any management fee in a year in which the fund's NAV declines"], 0, "Investors often back a fund because of specific individuals. A key person clause protects them by triggering suspension or other remedies if those people depart or stop devoting enough time."], ["Modern Portfolio Theory and Capital Market Theory", "Adding a new asset to a portfolio will reduce portfolio risk the most when its correlation with the existing portfolio is:", ["Exactly +1", "Slightly positive", "Strongly negative", "Equal to the asset's beta"], 2, "The lower the correlation, the greater the diversification benefit. Perfect positive correlation gives no risk reduction, while negative correlation lets losses in one holding be offset by gains in the other."], ["Governance of Funds and Due Diligence", "During investment due diligence, an investor finds that the fund's track record comes from a previous employer. The investor should check:", ["Only that the returns were above the Nifty 50", "Whether the manager has a large social media following and is often quoted in the financial press", "That the PPM contains the word 'guaranteed'", "Whether the record is attributable to this manager and verified independently"], 3, "Portable track records may include work by other team members or different conditions. Investors should verify attribution and accuracy rather than accept headline numbers."], ["Indices and Benchmarking", "An index provider caps the weight of any single stock in a sector index. The main purpose of such capping is to:", ["Limit concentration so the index stays diversified", "Guarantee that the index will rise faster than the broad market every year", "Give the largest company a bigger weight than its free-float market value", "Remove the need for periodic index rebalancing or reconstitution"], 0, "Without caps, one or two dominant companies can drive a sector index, making it concentrated and harder for funds to replicate within their own limits. Capping keeps the index diversified; it does not promise returns or remove rebalancing."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "Compared with an equity mutual fund, a long-only Category III AIF typically allows the manager to:", ["Offer the scheme to retail investors with a ₹500 SIP", "Guarantee a minimum return to investors each year", "Avoid all disclosure of fees to investors", "Run a more concentrated portfolio with fewer diversification limits"], 3, "Mutual funds face strict concentration limits for retail protection. Category III AIFs, aimed at sophisticated investors, can hold more concentrated positions, but still disclose fees and cannot guarantee returns."]]};
